{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.6.6","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"source":"<a href=\"https://www.kaggle.com/code/rizviabiha/toolcalling?scriptVersionId=367207161\" target=\"_blank\"><img align=\"left\" alt=\"Kaggle\" title=\"Open in Kaggle\" src=\"https://kaggle.com/static/images/open-in-kaggle.svg\"></a>","metadata":{},"cell_type":"markdown"},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Tool Calling in LangChain\n\n## 1. What is Tool Calling?\n\n**Tool Calling** is the process in which a Large Language Model (LLM) decides whether it needs an external tool (function) to answer a user's query.\n\nIf a tool is required, the LLM generates a structured output containing:\n\n1. The name of the tool it wants to use.\n2. The arguments required by that tool.\n3. The values of those arguments.\n\nFor example, suppose we have provided a multiplication tool to our LLM.\n\nIf the user asks:\n\n- \"How are you?\" → The LLM can answer on its own. No tool is required.\n- \"What is 8 multiplied by 7?\" → The LLM recognizes that it has access to a multiplication tool and generates a tool call.\n\nThe LLM does not actually execute the Python function. It only suggests which tool should be called and provides the required arguments.\n\n**The actual execution is handled by the programmer or the LangChain execution framework.**\n\n### Example of a Tool Call\n\nUser query:\n\n\"What is 8 multiplied by 7?\"\n\nThe LLM generates a structured tool call:\n\n```json\n{\n    \"name\": \"multiply\",\n    \"args\": {\n        \"a\": 8,\n        \"b\": 7\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- `name`: Name of the tool to execute.\n- `args`: Arguments that need to be passed to the tool.\n- `a` and `b`: Input parameters expected by the multiplication function.\n\nThe programmer receives this structured output and executes the corresponding Python function.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Three Important Concepts\n\nTool Calling involves three important steps:\n\n1. Tool Creation\n2. Tool Binding\n3. Tool Calling and Tool Execution\n\n### Step 1: Tool Creation\n\nTool creation means defining a Python function that the LLM can use as an external tool.\n\nIn LangChain, we can use the `@tool` decorator to convert an ordinary Python function into a LangChain tool.\n\nExample:\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_core.tools import tool\n\n@tool\ndef multiply(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Given 2 numbers a and b this tool returns their product\"\"\"\n    return a * b\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- `@tool` converts the Python function into a LangChain tool.\n- `multiply` is the tool name.\n- `a` and `b` are the input arguments.\n- `int` specifies their expected data types.\n- The docstring describes what the tool does.\n- `return a * b` contains the actual implementation.\n\nWe can inspect the tool:\n\n```python\nprint(multiply.name)\nprint(multiply.description)\nprint(multiply.args)\n```\n\nThese properties provide information about the tool:\n\n| Property | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `name` | Name of the tool |\n| `description` | Explains what the tool does |\n| `args` | Describes the expected input arguments and their types |\n\nWe can also execute the tool directly:\n\n```python\nmultiply.invoke({\n    'a': 3,\n    'b': 4\n})\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n12\n```\n\nNotice that here we are directly executing the tool ourselves. We are not asking the LLM to decide whether to use it.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Tool Binding\n\n**Tool Binding is the process of registering tools with an LLM so that the LLM knows which tools are available to it.**\n\nWhen we bind tools to an LLM, the model receives information about:\n\n1. How many tools are available.\n2. What each tool does (through its description).\n3. What input arguments each tool expects (through its schema).\n4. The expected data types and structure of those arguments.\n\nFor example:\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI\n\nllm = ChatOpenAI()\n\nllm_with_tools = llm.bind_tools([multiply])\n```\n\nHere:\n\n- `llm` is our language model.\n- `multiply` is the tool we created.\n- `bind_tools()` registers the tool with the model.\n- `llm_with_tools` is the model configured with the available tool definitions.\n\n**Important:** Tool binding does not execute the tool. It only makes the tool definitions available to the LLM.\n\nThe model can now decide whether a particular user query requires the multiplication tool.\n\n#### What happens after Tool Binding?\n\nSuppose we have registered only one tool:\n\n```python\nllm_with_tools = llm.bind_tools([multiply])\n```\n\nNow consider two user queries.\n\n**Case 1: Normal conversation**\n\n```python\nresponse = llm_with_tools.invoke(\"Hi, how are you?\")\nprint(response.content)\n```\n\nThe LLM can generate a normal conversational response without using any tool.\n\n**Case 2: Mathematical query**\n\n```python\nresponse = llm_with_tools.invoke(\n    \"Can you multiply 3 with 1000?\"\n)\n```\n\nThe LLM recognizes that multiplication is required and that a multiplication tool is available.\n\nIt generates a structured tool call containing:\n\n```python\n{\n    \"name\": \"multiply\",\n    \"args\": {\n        \"a\": 3,\n        \"b\": 1000\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWe can inspect the generated tool calls:\n\n```python\nresponse.tool_calls\n```\n\nThe LLM has identified the appropriate tool and generated its arguments, but the multiplication function has not yet been executed.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Tool Calling vs Tool Execution\n\nThese two concepts are closely related, but they are not the same.\n\n### Tool Calling\n\nTool Calling is the stage where the LLM decides:\n\n- Which tool should be used?\n- What arguments should be passed?\n- What values should those arguments contain?\n\nThe LLM generates a structured tool call.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nCall the multiply tool with:\na = 8\nb = 7\n```\n\n### Tool Execution\n\nTool Execution is the stage where the actual Python function is executed using the arguments suggested by the LLM.\n\nFor example:\n\n```python\nmultiply.invoke({\n    'a': 8,\n    'b': 7\n})\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n56\n```\n\n### Difference\n\n| Tool Calling | Tool Execution |\n|---|---|\n| Performed by the LLM | Performed by the programmer or execution framework |\n| Decides which tool to use | Runs the selected function |\n| Generates structured arguments | Passes arguments to the function |\n| Produces a tool call | Produces a tool result |\n\n**Remember:** The LLM does not automatically execute Python functions merely because tools have been bound to it.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Complete Tool Calling Workflow\n\nLet's understand the complete workflow.\n\nSuppose the user asks:\n\n\"Can you multiply 3 with 1000?\"\n\n### Step 1: User sends a query\n\n```python\nquery = HumanMessage(\n    'Can you multiply 3 with 1000'\n)\n```\n\n### Step 2: Create the messages list\n\n```python\nmessages = [query]\n```\n\nThe messages list maintains the conversation history.\n\n### Step 3: Send the query to the LLM\n\n```python\nresult = llm_with_tools.invoke(messages)\n```\n\nThe LLM checks the available tools and determines whether a tool is required.\n\nSince multiplication is required, it generates a tool call.\n\n### Step 4: Append the AI response\n\n```python\nmessages.append(result)\n```\n\nThe AI response contains the tool call generated by the LLM.\n\nWe can inspect it:\n\n```python\nresult.tool_calls\n```\n\n### Step 5: Execute the tool\n\n```python\ntool_result = multiply.invoke(\n    result.tool_calls[0]\n)\n```\n\nThe programmer executes the selected tool using the arguments generated by the LLM.\n\nThe tool returns the result:\n\n```text\n3000\n```\n\n### Step 6: Append the Tool Message\n\n```python\nmessages.append(tool_result)\n```\n\nThe tool result is added to the conversation history.\n\nA tool message contains the output returned by the executed tool.\n\n### Step 7: Send the updated conversation back to the LLM\n\n```python\nfinal_response = llm_with_tools.invoke(messages)\n\nprint(final_response.content)\n```\n\nNow the LLM receives the tool result and can formulate a natural-language response.\n\nPossible output:\n\n```text\n3 multiplied by 1000 is 3000.\n```\n\n### Complete Workflow Diagram\n\n```text\n          User Query\n               |\n               v\n        LLM with Tools\n               |\n               v\n       Does it need a tool?\n               |\n          +----+----+\n          |         |\n         No        Yes\n          |         |\n          v         v\n    Normal Answer  Tool Calling\n                    |\n                    v\n             Structured Output\n             (Tool Name + Args)\n                    |\n                    v\n             Tool Execution\n          (Programmer / LangChain)\n                    |\n                    v\n              Tool Message\n              (Tool Result)\n                    |\n                    v\n           Send Result to LLM\n                    |\n                    v\n            Final LLM Response\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Practical Implementation: Multiplication Tool\n\n### Complete Code\n\n```python\nfrom langchain_openai import ChatOpenAI\nfrom langchain_core.tools import tool\nfrom langchain_core.messages import HumanMessage\n\n# Tool Creation\n\n@tool\ndef multiply(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Given 2 numbers a and b this tool returns their product\"\"\"\n    return a * b\n\n# Tool Inspection\n\nprint(multiply.name)\nprint(multiply.description)\nprint(multiply.args)\n\n# Direct Tool Execution\n\nprint(multiply.invoke({'a': 3, 'b': 4}))\n\n# Initialize LLM\n\nllm = ChatOpenAI()\n\n# Tool Binding\n\nllm_with_tools = llm.bind_tools([multiply])\n\n# Create User Query\n\nquery = HumanMessage(\n    'Can you multiply 3 with 1000'\n)\n\nmessages = [query]\n\n# Tool Calling\n\nresult = llm_with_tools.invoke(messages)\n\n# Append AI Message\n\nmessages.append(result)\n\n# Tool Execution\n\ntool_result = multiply.invoke(result.tool_calls[0])\n\n# Append Tool Message\n\nmessages.append(tool_result)\n\n# Final LLM Response\n\nfinal_response = llm_with_tools.invoke(messages)\n\nprint(final_response.content)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 6. Using Multiple Tools Together\n\nAn LLM can have access to multiple tools.\n\nFor example, consider a currency conversion application.\n\nWe want to perform two operations:\n\n1. Fetch the conversion rate between two currencies.\n2. Calculate the converted currency amount.\n\nFor this, we will create two tools:\n\n- `get_conversion_factor`\n- `convert`\n\n### Tool 1: Get Conversion Factor\n\nThis tool fetches the conversion rate using an external exchange-rate API.\n\n```python\nimport requests\nfrom langchain_core.tools import tool\n\n@tool\ndef get_conversion_factor(\n    base_currency: str,\n    target_currency: str\n) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    This function fetches the currency conversion factor\n    between a given base currency and a target currency.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    url = (\n        f'https://v6.exchangerate-api.com/v6/'\n        f'{EXCHANGE_RATE_API_KEY}/pair/'\n        f'{base_currency}/{target_currency}'\n    )\n\n    response = requests.get(url)\n    response.raise_for_status()\n\n    return response.json()\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```python\nget_conversion_factor.invoke({\n    'base_currency': 'USD',\n    'target_currency': 'INR'\n})\n```\n\nThis tool returns exchange-rate information from the API.\n\n### Tool 2: Convert Currency\n\nThe second tool performs the actual mathematical calculation.\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Annotated\nfrom langchain_core.tools import tool, InjectedToolArg\n\n@tool\ndef convert(\n    base_currency_value: int,\n    conversion_rate: Annotated[\n        float,\n        InjectedToolArg\n    ]\n) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a currency conversion rate, this function\n    calculates the target currency value from\n    a given base currency value.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    return base_currency_value * conversion_rate\n```\n\nHere we introduce an important concept:\n\n### What is InjectedToolArg?\n\n`InjectedToolArg` is used to identify an argument that should be supplied by the application/programmer rather than generated by the LLM.\n\nConsider:\n\n```python\nconversion_rate: Annotated[\n    float,\n    InjectedToolArg\n]\n```\n\nThe `conversion_rate` argument is injected into the tool at execution time.\n\nWhy?\n\nBecause the conversion rate is not something the LLM should guess or invent. It must come from the output of the first tool.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The LLM provides the amount to convert.\n- The first tool fetches the actual conversion rate.\n- The programmer passes that rate to the second tool.\n\nThe injected argument is excluded from the tool schema presented to the LLM.\n\n### Direct Execution\n\n```python\nconvert.invoke({\n    'base_currency_value': 10,\n    'conversion_rate': 85.16\n})\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n851.6\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Binding Multiple Tools to an LLM\n\nNow we bind both tools to the LLM.\n\n```python\nllm = ChatOpenAI()\n\nllm_with_tools = llm.bind_tools([\n    get_conversion_factor,\n    convert\n])\n```\n\nThe LLM now knows about both tools.\n\nIt understands:\n\n| Tool | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `get_conversion_factor` | Fetches the exchange rate |\n| `convert` | Calculates the converted amount |\n\n### User Query\n\n```python\nmessages = [\n    HumanMessage(\n        'What is the conversion factor between INR and USD, '\n        'and based on that can you convert 10 INR to USD?'\n    )\n]\n```\n\nNow send the query to the LLM:\n\n```python\nai_message = llm_with_tools.invoke(messages)\n\nmessages.append(ai_message)\n```\n\nInspect the generated tool calls:\n\n```python\nai_message.tool_calls\n```\n\nThe LLM can identify that it needs the conversion-rate tool and the conversion tool.\n\nHowever, the second tool depends on the output of the first tool.\n\nThis is where the programmer must manage the execution order and pass the intermediate result.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Passing the Output of One Tool to Another\n\nThis is an important practical example of tool orchestration.\n\nWe first execute the conversion-rate tool, extract its result, and then pass that result to the conversion tool.\n\n```python\nimport json\n\nfor tool_call in ai_message.tool_calls:\n\n    # Execute the first tool\n    if tool_call['name'] == 'get_conversion_factor':\n\n        tool_message1 = get_conversion_factor.invoke(\n            tool_call\n        )\n\n        # Extract conversion rate\n        conversion_rate = json.loads(\n            tool_message1.content\n        )['conversion_rate']\n\n        # Append tool result\n        messages.append(tool_message1)\n\n    # Execute the second tool\n    if tool_call['name'] == 'convert':\n\n        # Inject conversion rate into arguments\n        tool_call['args']['conversion_rate'] = conversion_rate\n\n        tool_message2 = convert.invoke(tool_call)\n\n        # Append tool result\n        messages.append(tool_message2)\n```\n\nFinally:\n\n```python\nfinal_response = llm_with_tools.invoke(messages)\n\nprint(final_response.content)\n```\n\n### Understanding the Execution\n\nSuppose the user asks:\n\n\"Convert 10 INR into USD.\"\n\nThe workflow is:\n\n**Step 1:** LLM identifies the required tools.\n\n**Step 2:** LLM generates tool calls.\n\n**Step 3:** Programmer executes `get_conversion_factor`.\n\n**Step 4:** The API returns the conversion rate.\n\n**Step 5:** Programmer extracts the `conversion_rate` from the first tool's output.\n\n**Step 6:** Programmer injects this rate into the arguments of `convert`.\n\n**Step 7:** The `convert` tool performs the calculation.\n\n**Step 8:** Both tool results are added to the conversation history.\n\n**Step 9:** The updated conversation is sent back to the LLM.\n\n**Step 10:** LLM generates the final answer.\n\n### Why is InjectedToolArg useful here?\n\nWithout injected arguments, the LLM would be responsible for generating the conversion rate as well.\n\nBut the conversion rate is dynamic external information.\n\nUsing `InjectedToolArg` allows the programmer to provide the actual value returned by the first tool.\n\nThis makes the workflow more reliable.\n\n---\n\n## 9. What is an Agent in LangChain?\n\nAn Agent is a system in which an LLM can decide which tools to use and determine the next action based on the available information.\n\nInstead of manually managing every tool call, an agent executor can manage the tool-selection and execution loop.\n\nAn agent generally performs the following operations:\n\n1. Receives the user query.\n2. Analyzes the query.\n3. Decides which tool is required.\n4. Generates the tool call.\n5. Executes the selected tool.\n6. Observes the result.\n7. Decides whether another tool is required.\n8. Generates the final response.\n\n### ReAct Pattern\n\nReAct stands for:\n\n**Reasoning + Acting**\n\nThe ReAct approach allows an agent to alternate between reasoning about a task and taking actions through tools.\n\nA simplified workflow:\n\n```text\nUser Query\n    |\n    v\nReasoning\n    |\n    v\nSelect Tool\n    |\n    v\nAction (Tool Execution)\n    |\n    v\nObservation (Tool Result)\n    |\n    v\nReasoning Again\n    |\n    v\nFinal Answer\n```\n\n### Agent Implementation\n\nThe original CampusX example uses:\n\n```python\nfrom langchain.agents import initialize_agent, AgentType\n\nagent_executor = initialize_agent(\n    tools=[\n        get_conversion_factor,\n        convert\n    ],\n    llm=llm,\n    agent=AgentType.STRUCTURED_CHAT_ZERO_SHOT_REACT_DESCRIPTION,\n    verbose=True\n)\n\nuser_query = \"Hi how are you?\"\n\nresponse = agent_executor.invoke({\n    \"input\": user_query\n})\n\nprint(response)\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n| Parameter | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `tools` | List of tools available to the agent |\n| `llm` | Language model used by the agent |\n| `agent` | Specifies the agent type and its behavior |\n| `verbose=True` | Displays intermediate execution logs |\n\n**Note:** `initialize_agent` and this particular agent type belong to LangChain's older agent API. They are useful for understanding the original lecture, but are legacy APIs. Modern LangChain applications generally use `create_agent` or LangGraph-based agent workflows.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Tool Binding vs Tool Calling vs Tool Execution vs Agent\n\nThese concepts are related but perform different responsibilities.\n\n| Concept | What happens? |\n|---|---|\n| Tool Creation | We define a Python function and convert it into a LangChain tool. |\n| Tool Binding | We register the available tools with the LLM. |\n| Tool Calling | LLM decides which tool to use and generates structured arguments. |\n| Tool Execution | The actual Python function is executed. |\n| Tool Message | The result returned by the tool is added to the conversation. |\n| Agent | Manages the decision-making and tool execution workflow. |\n\n## 11. Important Points to Remember\n\n1. A tool is essentially a Python function that an LLM can use through a tool-calling interface.\n2. The `@tool` decorator converts a Python function into a LangChain tool.\n3. The tool's docstring provides its description.\n4. The function parameters and type annotations help define the input schema.\n5. `bind_tools()` registers tools with the LLM.\n6. Binding tools does not execute them.\n7. The LLM decides whether a tool is necessary for a particular query.\n8. A tool call contains the tool name and its arguments.\n9. The LLM generally does not execute the Python function itself.\n10. The programmer or execution framework executes the selected tool.\n11. Tool execution produces a result that can be returned as a Tool Message.\n12. The Tool Message is added to the conversation history.\n13. The updated conversation is sent back to the LLM to generate the final response.\n14. `InjectedToolArg` allows the application to supply arguments that should not be generated by the LLM.\n15. Multiple tools can be connected to accomplish a complex task.\n16. An Agent can manage iterative tool selection and execution.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Quick Revision\n\n**Tool Creation:** Define a Python function and convert it into a tool.\n\n**Tool Binding:** Register the tools with the LLM so it knows their names, descriptions and input schemas.\n\n**Tool Calling:** LLM decides which tool is required and generates a structured output containing the tool name and arguments.\n\n**Tool Execution:** Programmer or LangChain executes the actual function using the generated arguments.\n\n**Tool Message:** Contains the result returned by the executed tool.\n\n**Agent:** Coordinates reasoning, tool selection, execution and observations to complete a task.\n\n### The Most Important Concept\n\nRemember this sequence:\n\n```text\nTool Creation\n      |\n      v\nTool Binding\n      |\n      v\nUser Query\n      |\n      v\nLLM Decision\n      |\n      v\nTool Calling\n      |\n      v\nStructured Tool Output\n      |\n      v\nTool Execution\n      |\n      v\nTool Message\n      |\n      v\nLLM Generates Final Answer\n```\n\n**Core takeaway:** The LLM decides WHAT tool should be used and WITH WHICH arguments. The programmer or execution framework is responsible for actually RUNNING that tool and returning its result to the LLM.","metadata":{}}]}